<!--
The render page, booting `beet-render.wasm`: the browser binary plus the windowed
render stack, running a wgpu scene (`examples/spatial/scene_3d.bsx`) in the tab.
Build it with `just build-wasm-render`; until then the page loads and the module
fetch 404s.

The runtime boots windowless like every beet binary, so the GPU rides WebGPU: the
entry probes `navigator.gpu` and requests an adapter, booting GPU-less when the
browser refuses one (WebGL2 cannot back a windowless boot, its adapters only
exist on a canvas-backed surface). Either way the whole render stack is live —
assets, cameras, scene systems — which the console output below proves; pixels in
the tab await the canvas design (a scene `<Window/>` has no winit backend here to
give it a surface).

Structurally identical to `/` (a full `<HtmlDocument>`, `<RenderConsole>` before
`<Wasm>` so the console wrap installs first), differing only in which artifact and
which program it names.
-->
<HtmlDocument>
	<Fragment slot="head">
		<Stylesheet/>
		<ColorSchemeScript/>
		<LiveReloadScript/>
	</Fragment>
	<h1>Rendering in the browser</h1>
	<p>
		A wgpu scene booting in your browser: the same render stack a native window
		runs, minus winit, driven by the awaited wasm runner. With WebGPU granted the
		scene runs on a real adapter; without it the render capabilities stay linked
		but GPU-less. Its console output renders below.
	</p>
	<p>
		The <a href="/">home page</a> boots the floor of the range,
		<code>beet-min.wasm</code>, and the <a href="/scripting">scripting page</a>
		boots the ceiling, <code>beet-full.wasm</code>, which carries this render
		stack and everything else that compiles for the browser.
	</p>
	<RenderConsole/>
	<MainBsx src="/examples/spatial/scene_3d.bsx"/>
	<Wasm src="/assets/wasm/beet-render.wasm"/>
</HtmlDocument>
